<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Athena (Paestum)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> is a Doric temple built around 500 BCE in the Greek colony of Poseidonia. Long misnamed the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a>, it stands on high ground in the north of the city and combines a Doric exterior with Ionic columns in its porch. It is one of the three standing Greek temples of Paestum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-athena-paestum.jpg" alt="Temple of Athena (Paestum)" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paestum_BW_2013-05-17_13-58-28.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
